# Introduction
modules.cpp is modern and minimal, self-contained codebase built with C++20 modules.
It describes, builds, tests, and documents itself from source.

The project has no third-party build system, runtime library, test framework,
package manager, or documentation generator. It requires a host C++20
compiler, the C++ standard library, and basic platform services. Today it
builds with GCC with C++ module support and a POSIX shell.

Every participating directory contains an mm.mdy manifest. These manifests
define the project tree, targets, source order, and module dependencies. The
repository contains the parser, graph model, compiler driver, test framework,
tools, applications, tests, documentation renderer, and bootstrap scripts.

The codebase supports test-driven development. A change can begin with a
focused failing test, proceed through a small implementation change, and end
with regression coverage and updated documentation.

# Design
The architecture separates declarations, reusable policy, and workflows.

- Manifests declare structure, targets, sources, and dependencies.
- Core modules implement parsing, building, and testing policy.
- Tools and applications expose focused command-line workflows.

Shared behavior belongs in a module. Front ends handle arguments, select a
workflow, and report results. This keeps policy reusable and interfaces small.

## Design goals
These goals describe the target architecture; see Current boundaries for
which of them are already enforced in the current implementation.

- Modular — use C++20 modules with explicit interfaces and dependencies.
- Stable APIs — publish versioned module contracts and evolve them compatibly.
- Manifest-driven — model targets and artifacts in versioned MDY graphs.
- Extensible — add components through the shared manifest and artifact model.
- Deterministic — derive build order from dependencies and source order.
- Inspectable — expose commands, relationships, decisions, and artifacts.
- Self-contained — require no third-party project-specific dependencies.
- Recoverable — bootstrap from source without existing generated artifacts.
- Self-hosting — use reusable C++ modules to rebuild project tools.
- Self-verifying — support TDD, regression tests, and expected failures.
- Self-documenting — build documentation from project manifests and MDY.
- Safe — confine traversal and quote paths passed to external processes.
- Portable — isolate compiler and operating-system behavior behind interfaces.

Stable and portable interfaces are design goals. The public code carries an
MDY format version, but does not yet enforce module API compatibility, and
the compiler and process driver remain GCC- and POSIX-oriented.

## Architectural model
The architecture has three cooperating layers.

- Manifests describe intent. The mm.mdy files form a rooted project tree and 
  declare targets, sources, and module dependencies.
- Core modules implement policy. The modules under modules/mm parse documents, build dependency graphs,
  drive compilation, and provide testing support.
- Tools and applications perform workflows. Small programs under tools and apps import the core modules
  and expose build, test, example, inspection, and documentation behavior.

## Core module dependency graph
mm.app, mm.mdy, and mm.test have no dependencies on other core modules.
mm.build imports mm.mdy because manifests are MDY documents.

    mm.app <-- main application
    mm.app <-- mdy application
    mm.mdy <-- mm.build
    mm.mdy <-- mdy application
    mm.build <-- build tool
    mm.build <-- test tool
    mm.build <-- mdy application
    mm.test (independent; used by test targets)

- the build tool imports mm.build.
- the test tool imports mm.build.
- the mdy application imports mm.app, mm.mdy, and mm.build.
- the main application imports mm.app.


# Repository layout
The source tree is organized by responsibility.

.
+-- mm.mdy                    project manifest
+-- bootstrap.sh              bootstrap build
+-- build.sh                  full project build
+-- test.sh                   smoke and target tests
+-- document.sh               HTML documentation
+-- check.sh                  optional: docs/modules-c++20.mdy via cppcheck
+-- clean.sh                  generated-file cleanup
+-- apps/
|   +-- main/                 minimal application
|   +-- mdy/                  MDY and documentation application
+-- modules/mm/
|   +-- app/                  mm.app
|   +-- build/                mm.build
|   +-- mdy/                  mm.mdy and white-box tests
|   +-- test/                 mm.test
+-- tools/
|   +-- build/                bootstrap and build front ends
|   +-- test/                 test front end
|   +-- check/                cppcheck front end and addon; see check.sh
+-- tests/mm/
|   +-- build/                mm.build tests
|   +-- mdy/                  mm.mdy public and regression tests
+-- docs/
|   +-- mm.mdy                documentation manifest
|   +-- mdy.mdy               MDY format reference
|   +-- modules.mdy           this guide
|   +-- modules-c++20.mdy     C++20 language subset specification
|
+-- out/                      all of the output and generated files
+-- gcm.cache/                gcc modules cache

Generated objects and programs live under out. GCC compiled-module state lives
under gcm.cache. Neither directory is part of the source model.

# MDY manifests
MDY is the line-oriented format used for manifests and prose. Every MDY file
is an MDY document; a manifest is an MDY document named mm.mdy that declares a
project or dir target. This guide is itself an MDY document (kind: doc), not a
manifest. See docs/mdy.mdy for MDY syntax, metadata rules, body blocks, usage,
and parser limitations.

For this guide, the important manifest fields are:

- folder connects a project or directory to a child directory.
- file lists a module or application source in compiler order.
- unit lists a test-owned translation unit in compiler order.
- module assigns an importable name to a module target.
- use declares a dependency on an importable module.

The supported target kinds are project, dir, module, app, test, and doc.
Project and dir targets organize the tree. The build tool compiles module and
app targets. The test tool builds test targets. The mdy app renders docs.

Repeated manifest values retain declaration order. Source order is explicit;
target order is calculated from use dependencies.

# Core modules
## mm.app
mm.app exports the App base class. App accepts argc and argv, provides a
virtual run function, and exposes a main-style forwarding member.

App stores no state, and run returns success.

## mm.mdy
mm.mdy exports the document model and parser:

- BlockType identifies headings, paragraphs, lists, and empty blocks.
- Block stores a type and its text.
- MDYDocument stores repeated metadata values and body blocks.
- Parser reads a complete MDY file or parses body lines.

mm.build uses this module to read manifests. apps/mdy uses it for inspection
and HTML rendering. Parsing remains independent from build policy.

Tests cover internal line parsing, public file parsing, regressions, and known
defects. The exact format contract is in docs/mdy.mdy.

## mm.build
mm.build contains the shared project and build model. Its main types are:

- Toolchain stores compiler commands, flags, and verbosity.
- Unit identifies a source path and optional module name.
- Target describes a module, application, test, or document.
- Tree separates build targets from discovered tests and documents.
- Node preserves manifest parent-child structure for documentation.

The manifest walker starts at a selected directory and follows folder entries.
Canonical paths detect cycles, repeated visits, symlink aliases, and paths that
escape the project root. Validation rejects missing manifests, unknown kinds,
unnamed targets, missing sources, and unnamed modules.

The graph layer resolves use entries against module names. A topological walk
places dependencies before consumers and rejects unknown modules and cycles.
order covers the full project. order_from selects one target and its reachable
dependencies. closure collects objects without repeating shared dependencies.

The execution layer compiles units, links programs, and installs applications.
Generated paths mirror source paths under out. shell_quote protects paths sent
through the shell. Child exit and signal states become tool exit codes.

Toolchain invokes c++ with C++20 and GCC modules-ts flags. A full build
clears gcm.cache before compiling.

## mm.test
mm.test provides the project test framework. It exports assertions, test cases,
suites, a static registrar, expected-failure support, and the test runner.

Tests are ordinary functions. Static registrars collect their suites before
main calls run_all. expect reports failures with source_location, so tests do
not need assertion macros.

An expected failure is reported as xfail and does not fail the run. If it starts
passing, it becomes xpass and fails the run until the stale marker is removed.
This turns known defects into executable records without weakening regressions.

# Tools
## build0
tools/build/main.cpp is compiled as out/build0 without importing project
modules. It creates a recovery boundary that only needs the host compiler.

bootstrap.sh uses build0 as a smoke-test artifact, then issues explicit
compiler commands to build mm.mdy, mm.build, and build1.

## build1 and build
tools/build/build.cpp is the manifest-driven build front end. bootstrap.sh
links it as out/build1. A full build also installs it as out/bin/build.

The tool resolves a manifest, enters its root, validates the project tree,
orders dependencies, compiles modules and applications, links programs, and
installs applications under out/bin.

Tests and documentation are discovered but remain separate workflows. This
allows code to be rebuilt without requiring tests or docs to run first.

Common commands are:

- ./build.sh
- ./out/build1
- ./out/bin/build mm.mdy
- ./out/bin/build -v modules/mm.mdy

## test
tools/test/main.cpp is installed as out/bin/test. It accepts one kind:test
manifest, finds the project root, and loads the project module graph.

The selected test target is appended to that graph. order_from selects only
the test and its transitive module dependencies. The tool compiles them under
out/tests, links the objects directly, runs the binary, and returns its status.

Direct object linking preserves static test registrars that an archive linker
might otherwise discard.

Common commands are:

- ./out/bin/test tests/mm/build/mm.mdy
- ./out/bin/test tests/mm/mdy/mm.mdy
- ./out/bin/test -v tests/mm/build

# Applications
## main
apps/main imports mm.app, constructs App, calls run, and returns its result.
The base implementation returns success and produces no output.

The executable is written to out/apps/main/main and installed as out/bin/main.

## mdy
apps/mdy imports mm.mdy and mm.build. It inspects MDY documents and generates
HTML documentation.

Verbose mode prints metadata and body blocks. The -s option creates and parses
a sample document for smoke testing. The -h option writes escaped HTML.

For a project or dir manifest, HTML mode calls mm.build::load_nodes and writes
one index.html page per reachable manifest. Output mirrors the manifest tree
and includes metadata, breadcrumbs, child links, and prose.

Common commands are:

- ./out/bin/mdy -s
- ./out/bin/mdy docs/modules.mdy -h > modules.html
- ./document.sh
- ./document.sh -o=site

The renderer supports MDY headings, paragraphs, and unordered lists. It is not
a Markdown engine and has no stylesheet, template, search, or asset pipeline.

# Build and development workflow
A clean repository can produce all project tools with this sequence:

- ./clean.sh
- ./bootstrap.sh
- ./build.sh
- ./test.sh
- ./document.sh

bootstrap.sh creates out/build0 and out/build1. build.sh uses build1 to build
the complete project. The installed out/bin/build can then rebuild the project,
which makes the toolchain self-hosting.

An optional, experimental step can follow test.sh:

- ./check.sh

check.sh enforces docs/modules-c++20.mdy over the manifest tree, using
tools/check to wrap the installed cppcheck tool. Unlike the rest of this
sequence, it depends on a third-party tool: cppcheck must already be
installed, and check.sh is not part of the self-hosting chain above. It is
therefore not required to build, test, or document the project, and skipping
it changes nothing else in this sequence.

For TDD, run the smallest relevant test target first:

- Add a case that defines the behavior or reproduces the defect.
- Run out/bin/test for the selected target and confirm the failure.
- Make the smallest implementation change that satisfies the case.
- Run the focused target until it passes.
- Run test.sh for full regression coverage.
- Update interfaces, manifests, and docs when their contracts change.

White-box tests may live with a module. Public integration and regression tests
live under tests. Expected failures are reserved for understood, deferred
defects and become ordinary regression tests when fixed.

# Generated artifacts
- out/modules contains module objects.
- out/apps contains application objects and executables.
- out/tools contains tool objects and executables.
- out/bin contains installed commands.
- out/tests contains isolated test builds.
- out/index.html and nested pages contain generated documentation.
- gcm.cache contains GCC compiled-module state.

# Current boundaries
These are the gaps between the Design goals and today's implementation.

- App defines the application boundary but stores no state; shared lifecycle,
  context, logging, or argument services can be added there without coupling
  them to build policy.
- The compiler driver follows GCC modules-ts conventions.
- Process execution and bootstrap scripts require POSIX services.
- Builds are full rebuilds rather than incremental builds.
- Source order is declared rather than inferred from import statements.
- use dependencies resolve only to module targets.
- Tests and documentation are separate build stages.
- MDY is intentionally smaller than YAML or Markdown.

These boundaries keep the core model small. Compiler adapters, incremental
scheduling, richer rendering, and broader platforms can evolve behind the
interfaces without changing the manifest-driven architecture.

# Glossary
Terms used above that are not standard C++ vocabulary.

- translation unit — one source file plus everything it imports, as the
  compiler sees it for a single compile step. Each .cpp or .cppm file listed
  in a manifest's file or unit entries is compiled as its own translation
  unit.
- module interface / .cppm — the file that declares export module and lists
  what a module exposes. Consumers import the module name; they do not read
  this file's text directly the way #include copies a header's text.
- static registrar — an object whose constructor runs before main, used here
  to record a test case or suite into a global registry without requiring
  the test file to be listed anywhere by hand. This is why mm.test tests are
  "ordinary functions" that still get discovered automatically.
- topological walk — visiting a dependency graph so that every node is
  visited only after all the nodes it depends on. mm.build uses this to
  compile dependencies before the modules that use them.
- closure (graph sense) — the full set of nodes reachable from a starting
  node by following dependency edges. Unrelated to a C++ lambda closure;
  mm.build's closure function computes this reachable set so shared
  dependencies are not compiled or linked twice.
- canonical path — a filesystem path with symlinks resolved and relative
  segments removed, so two different-looking paths that name the same file
  compare equal. mm.build uses this to detect cycles and repeated visits
  when walking manifests.
- shell injection / shell_quote — shell injection is when untrusted text
  (such as a path) is passed to a shell command and interpreted as extra
  commands instead of plain data. shell_quote escapes a path so it is always
  treated as data when mm.build passes it to an external process.
- GCC modules-ts — the compiler flag family (-fmodules-ts) GCC currently
  uses for C++20 module support. It predates GCC's later, more standard
  module support and is one reason the compiler driver is described as
  GCC-oriented rather than portable across compilers today.
- front end (tool) — a small program (build, test, mdy) that parses
  arguments and calls into a core module to do the real work. Not to be
  confused with a compiler front end, which parses source code.
- expected failure / xfail / xpass — an expected failure is a test known to
  fail today; mm.test reports it as xfail instead of failing the run. If a
  marked test starts passing, mm.test reports it as xpass and fails the run
  until the stale marker is removed, so fixed defects cannot be forgotten.
